  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather will prevail through Tuesday night.\n\nHigh pressure over the area will sink to our south to the Mid-\nAtlantic region in response to a weakening backdoor front dropping \nout of Canada. While most of the area will continue to see dry \nweather today, an isolated shower can't be ruled out across the St. \nLawrence Valley and areas east of Lake Ontario. \n\nOtherwise...fairly nice weather will continue with partly sunny \nskies and highs in the 70s. \n\nTonight...more of the same dry weather expected to continue in \nadvance of an approaching cold frontal system. Not quite as cool \nwith overnight lows in the upper 50s to low-mid 60s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A cold front will cross the eastern Great Lakes \nWednesday through Wednesday night, producing showers and \nthunderstorms.\n\nA mid level shortwave trough cuts across Ontario and Quebec \nWednesday through Wednesday night, with its trailing cold front \nworking across the eastern Great Lakes region. Deep rich moisture \nand large scale forcing for ascent will support some showers and \nscattered thunderstorms development in the late afternoon and into \nthe evening hours. Given the high PWAT (1.9-2.1 inches) environment \nany stronger storms may be capable of producing torrential \ndownpours, although as was noted storms should move right along and \nkeep the flood risk low. As far as the severe storm potential, \nforecast guidance shows very weak instability (500-1000 J/kg) but \ndecent 0-6KM bulk shear values of 35-45 knots. This may be enough to \nsupport a few stronger storms but again the overall lake of stronger \ninstability will likely hold down the severe weather risk. \n\nWith the passage of the cold front showers and thunderstorms will \ndiminish in coverage but a few showers may linger Thursday. Dry \nweather returns Thursday night and Friday as high pressure builds \ninto the eastern Great Lakes.",
